.TH CHMOD 1 "February 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
chmod \- change file mode bits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chmod
.RB [ -R " [" -H " | " -L " | " -P ]]
.RB [ -dfh ]
.I mode
.I file
.RI ...
.PP
.B chmod
.RB [ -R " [" -H " | " -L " | " -P ]]
.RB [ -dfh ]
.BI --reference= rfile
.I file
.RI ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B chmod
utility changes mode bits on each specified
.IR file .
Modes may be numeric (octal) or symbolic.
.PP
Numeric modes set permissions directly (for example,
.BR 755 ).
Symbolic modes are evaluated with setmode/getmode semantics (for example,
.BR u+rwx,g-w ).
.PP
When
.B --reference
is used,
.B chmod
copies mode bits from the reference file's
.B st_mode
permissions.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -R
Recurse into directories.
.TP
.BR -H
When used with
.BR -R ,
follow symbolic links given on the command line, but do not follow other
symbolic links encountered during traversal.
.TP
.BR -L
When used with
.BR -R ,
follow all symbolic links during traversal.
.TP
.BR -P
Do not follow symbolic links during recursive traversal (default recursive
policy).
.TP
.BR --reference= RFILE
Use mode bits from
.I RFILE
instead of a mode operand.
.TP
.BR -d
Change mode on directories only.
Non-directory entries are skipped.
.TP
.BR -f
Suppress most diagnostics. Errors still cause a nonzero exit status.
.TP
.BR -h
Attempt to change the mode of symbolic links themselves instead of their
targets.
.SH SYMBOLIC MODES
Symbolic modes support:
.TP
.B who
One or more of
.BR u ,
.BR g ,
.BR o ,
.BR a .
.TP
.B operation
One of
.BR + ,
.BR - ,
.BR = .
.TP
.B permissions
Any combination of
.BR r ,
.BR w ,
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
.BR s ,
.BR t ,
.BR u ,
.BR g ,
.BR o .
.PP
The mode parser accepts comma-separated clauses and applies them in order.
.SH ERROR HANDLING
On per-path failure,
.B chmod
records failure, continues processing remaining operands, and exits nonzero.
Error messages include the program name and target path unless
.B -f
is specified.
.SH PORTABILITY NOTES
On platforms where changing symbolic-link mode bits is unsupported,
.B -h
fails for symbolic links with a clear diagnostic and nonzero status.
No destructive fallback is used.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All requested mode changes succeeded (or were intentionally skipped by
.BR -d ).
.TP
.B 1
One or more operations failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B chmod 755 file
Set file permissions to rwxr-xr-x.
.TP
.B chmod u=rwX,g+rX,o-rwx -R project
Recursively grant user read/write and conditional execute, grant group
read/execute, and clear other permissions.
.TP
.B chmod -d 700 dir1 dir2 file
Change only directories
.B dir1
and
.BR dir2 ;
skip
.BR file .
.TP
.B chmod --reference=template.conf target.conf
Apply mode bits from
.B template.conf
to
.BR target.conf .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stat (2),
.BR lstat (2),
.BR readlink (2),
.BR chown (1)
